    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d338">

                <pb facs="0388" n="376" sameAs="frus:frus1943v02/pg376"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 European War 1939/9–2543:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The British Prime Minister</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Churchill</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to President Roosevelt </hi><anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d338-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1943-09-24">September 24, 1943</date>.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>421. My No. 420 of September 24th. Macmillan now tells me he is confident that
                    Badoglio’s signature can be obtained to whole set of terms within the next few
                    days and that the longer we leave it the more haggling there will be. It may be
                    some time before the new Commission can give their views and I should myself
                    feel happier if we clinched the matter now. This might save us a great deal of
                    trouble later on.</p>
                <list>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>At Eisenhower’s suggestion we have made the preamble less harsh. We have
                        also provided that armistice of September 3rd will remain operative.</item>
                    <label>3.</label>
                    <item>See also Uncle Joe <hi rendition="#italic">passim</hi>.<anchor n="2"
                            xml:id="d338-f2"/>
                    </item>
                </list>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
